## RANKING
1) PR-03 — clears the filed HIGH single blocker at low cost; incident-backed instrument repair; an unchanged-conclusion NULL is genuinely informative.
2) PR-05 — the only fresh-cluster held-out design with preregistered predictions; cleanest replication value in the set.
3) PR-07 — adjudicates the surviving reviewer attack with the strictest NULL rule; accept its non-confirmatory label.
4) PR-04 — the only mechanism-level out-of-distribution test and the strongest held-out design; MED cost and (if M-stage) gated on PR-03.
5) PR-01 — gates the paper's equality claim (E2); high payoff once the score-evaluation-point defect is fixed.

(PR-08 is the best first-round backup if PR-01's feasibility gate fails; PR-02 is a useful descriptive second-round item; PR-06 is out.)

## STRONGEST_OBJECTION
Shared-evidence-base circularity: six of eight candidates (PR-01, 02, 03, 06, 07, 08) re-derive conclusions from the same frozen carriers and tables that generated the findings they repair, so "held-out" is only cluster-level resampling of a corpus whose structure already fixed the results (E3's 1.0 ceiling, E1's family rates) — hypothesis generation and test are not separated, and the portfolio reads as eight independent confirmations when it is at most two out-of-sample replications plus several re-analyses. It most threatens PR-06 (the 1.0 ceiling makes non-inferiority definitional, which is why it fails above) and PR-07 (already conceded non-confirmatory), and it caps the evidential weight that PR-02, PR-03, and PR-08 can carry no matter how clean their internals are. Only PR-05 (fresh clusters) and PR-04 (new mechanism) escape the objection, which is why both must be in round one and why any first-round claim set must be framed as "repairs plus one out-of-sample replication."

